Test ID: IR-09
Category: Source Reliability
Target: WebCrawler (date labelling)
Objective: Does the crawler stamp today's date on historical pages?
Setup/Input: search_and_crawl('Apollo 11 moon landing Wikipedia')
Expected secure behaviour: Real page date or blank.
Actual behaviour: today=2026-10-03; results=[('Apollo 11 - Wikipedia', '2026-10-03'), ('Moon landing - Wikipedia', '2026-10-03'), ('Apollo 11', '2026-10-03')]
Evidence/Log:
date assignment code: L234: pub_date = parsed.strftime("%Y-%m-%d"); L238: pub_date = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d", time.gmtime()); L259: today = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d", time.gmtime()); L284: today = time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d", time.gmtime()); L368: date_str = published_date or time.strftime("%Y-%m-%d", time.gmtime())
Outcome: Vulnerability confirmed
Impact 2 x Likelihood 4 = 8 -> Severity: Low
Technical explanation: A date-labelling bug (not attacker-controlled) that removes recency signal from the verifier and audit trail.
Mitigation: Parse <meta article:published_time>/Last-Modified or leave the date empty.
